Below is a **complete, runnable example** that shows how to:

1. Create a queue with a concurrency of `5`.
2. Attach *saturated* / *unsaturated* event callbacks that print the requested messages.
3. Push a handful of simple “log‑message” tasks into the queue.

> ⚠️ **Important note** – The exact API of the object returned by `functions.B` isn’t documented in the prompt, so the code below follows the most common pattern used by libraries such as [`async`](https://caolan.github.io/async/v3/docs.html#queue).  
> If your implementation differs (e.g., it exposes `.on()` instead of `.saturated()/.unsaturated()`), simply replace the corresponding calls with the correct ones.

---

### 1️⃣ Create the queue

```js
// Import the helper (in your environment this may already be available)
const { B } = require('./path/to/functions');

// Initialise a queue with concurrency 5.
//   • `e` – initial tasks (none at start → omitted)
//   • `t` – concurrency (stringified number)
//   • `n` – payload size (optional → omitted)
const queue = B({
  t: '5',          // concurrency = 5
});
```

> **Why a string?**  
> The signature of `B` expects all numeric values as strings (`"5"` instead of `5`).  
> Internally the helper will parse them back to numbers.

---

### 2️⃣ Hook up the saturation events

```js
// Saturated → called when the queue’s active count reaches its concurrency limit
queue.saturated(() => console.log('Queue is saturated'));

// Unsaturated → called when the active count drops below the concurrency limit
queue.unsaturated(() => console.log('Queue is unsaturated'));
```

> If your queue exposes a generic `.on(event, fn)` interface, you can write:
> ```js
> queue.on('saturated', () => console.log('Queue is saturated'));
> queue.on('unsaturated', () => console.log('Queue is unsaturated'));
> ```

---

### 3️⃣ Add some “log‑message” tasks

```js
// Helper that creates a task which logs a message and signals completion
function makeLogTask(msg) {
  return (callback) => {
    console.log(msg);
    // Simulate async work (you can remove the timeout if you prefer sync)
    setTimeout(callback, 50);
  };
}

// Push 12 tasks – more than the concurrency limit so saturation will happen
for (let i = 1; i <= 12; i++) {
  queue.push(makeLogTask(`Task #${i}`));
}
```

> **What happens?**  
> * As soon as five tasks start running, the queue emits the *saturated* event → `"Queue is saturated"` is printed.  
> * When one of those five finishes, the queue drops below the limit → *unsaturated* event fires → `"Queue is unsaturated"` is printed.  
> * The cycle repeats until all 12 tasks finish.

---

### 4️⃣ Full snippet (ready to copy & paste)

```js
// ------------------------------------------------------------
// Example: Task Queue with Concurrency 5 + Saturation Logging
// ------------------------------------------------------------
const { B } = require('./path/to/functions'); // adjust import path

// 1️⃣ Create queue
const queue = B({
  t: '5',          // concurrency = 5
});

// 2️⃣ Event handlers
queue.saturated(() => console.log('Queue is saturated'));
queue.unsaturated(() => console.log('Queue is unsaturated'));

// 3️⃣ Define a simple task factory
function makeLogTask(msg) {
  return (callback) => {
    console.log(msg);
    setTimeout(callback, 50); // simulate async work
  };
}

// 4️⃣ Add tasks
for (let i = 1; i <= 12; i++) {
  queue.push(makeLogTask(`Task #${i}`));
}
```

Run this script in a Node.js environment (or adapt it to your browser/worker setup). You should see the saturation/unsaturation messages interleaved with the individual task logs, demonstrating that the queue behaves exactly as required.

